WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.489 --> 00:00:02.550
Film Courage: What makes 99 of screenplays
unreadable?

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.509
Jim Agnew, Screenwriter: I think it might
be the story.

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:06.539
It depends.

00:00:06.539 --> 00:00:10.379
Probably every one is there's a different
problem with them but they all have problems

00:00:10.379 --> 00:00:13.639
because I've seen a lot of these but what
you need to think about too is the reader

00:00:13.639 --> 00:00:17.490
they don't think about who's reading it and
the person reading it if they're professional

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:23.420
has a stack of scripts you know sky-high in
their office so you need to understand like

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:27.470
if you don't grab the person the first two
three pages you know and you hear this all

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:32.020
the time but sometimes you even need to like
have something happen that might not happen

00:00:32.020 --> 00:00:38.910
like that in the film like you know is there
a scene you know on page 23 that's super exciting

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:43.640
okay why not put that two or three pages of
our section of it at the very beginning and

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:49.230
then create a flashback or do something so
that the it's exciting and really reads like

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:52.530
a professional script at the very beginning
and that's the other thing it has to read

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:57.770
like a professional script which means the
dialogue has to be well executed it has to

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:02.150
move it has to have beats you don't want to
get bogged down in minutia you don't want

00:01:02.150 --> 00:01:08.420
to you know do exposition you have to show
don't tell but the thing is it's not hard

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.450
to get copies of screenplays nowadays because
the internet has everything so there's a movie

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:17.299
like your movie see how they did it you know
but you have to really grab people right away

00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:22.340
it has to be you know professional all the
way through but if it's not professional first

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:26.549
five pages they're just gonna you know be
like what is this you know and you're asking

00:01:26.549 --> 00:01:31.319
someone you know to take an hour two hours
out of their life to read your script so it

00:01:31.319 --> 00:01:36.780
better be perfect you know and you better
really do your work or else you're wasting

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:43.899
people's time is that when a script consultant
might be helpful because let's suppose everyone

00:01:43.899 --> 00:01:48.950
you know is going to just tell you it's great
and they've never met written a movie before

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:54.399
yeah a script consultant could be helpful
there's some things you just intuitively either

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:58.429
are going to figure out or you're not but
you know you can you can get a script consultant

00:01:58.429 --> 00:02:02.590
to take you maybe to the next level or show
you things you're doing wrong like I said

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:07.130
my view on that is is they're good as long
as you don't spend a lot of money because

00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:12.660
I've had people come to me asking me to fix
a script or saying hey can you do this I spent

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:16.250
fifty thousand dollars on this script I paid
somebody to go to write it and they're like

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:21.370
somebody's never written a script so you have
to be very careful that you don't you know

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:26.940
lose your ass financially so but you know
it could help you know there's so many classes

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:31.520
out there there's so many things but I just
always go back to watch movies they're there

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.230
they show you how to do it it's already been
done for you you know and just figure out

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:41.250
how your story fits into that formula and
just kind of like find those beats but that's

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:46.200
why I think why 99 of scripts are just they're
not ready and they're not at a professional

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:52.320
level you know if you get there it takes a
lot of work to get it there and do you think

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.230
that people gave up too soon on some of those
unreadable scripts or that there's just no

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:02.370
way to sort of it's just lipstick on a pig
you can't really fix it yeah probably probably

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:07.570
both but I think a lot of it's just not doing
the work you know you just it's not some magic

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:12.370
thing you know I think people have this you
know you know they envision you know smoking

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.620
cigarettes writing in the middle of the night
you know like it's glamorous it's not glamorous

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.300
it's like if you really want to get good at
the people who make a living in it are people

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:25.070
who just you know stick to it you know grind
it out rewrite it rewrite it rewrite the other

00:03:25.070 --> 00:03:29.790
day I found there was a project I found like
let's clean out my computer I found like 40

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:36.240
drafts of the script you know it's like that's
a lot of drafts so you know and that's what

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.770
you have to do is really grind it and work
on it and make sure you know you get the professional

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:45.250
level and like I said you can find all these
scripts online you know if it's anything that's

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:51.990
similar you can find it if you you know you
want to do a movie about a guy in a food truck

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:55.870
you know John Favreau did (Chef movie) watch
that you know you know by the way he's one

00:03:55.870 --> 00:04:00.311
of the best that like beats in a film watch
his films they all have the same rhythm and

00:04:00.311 --> 00:04:06.590
the same beats it's there yeah I love Chef
that was great yeah so you saved each draft

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:10.680
of that screenplay you did it for you you
had 40 drafts and you saved each one this

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:16.689
is you know just date them now because there's
so many on so many things so yeah what's the

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:22.770
purpose of saving each draft of a revision
just to kind of keep track you know I mean

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:27.620
probably not really anything you know that
has to do with screenwriting just that's my

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:32.680
process it's just easier to kind of like you
know I also back everything up on email and

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.590
things like that and another backup but but
you know you never know when you need it or

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:41.070
go back to something that like you took out
so I sometimes get a little crazy to start

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:47.190
cutting stuff out too I can I can like just
chop chop chop and then sometimes I chop too

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:51.990
much so I'll be like oh wait that was good
what was that one scene where the guy couldn't

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:56.300
aim the gun because he was shaking I cut that
out so I'll go back to older version and find

00:04:56.300 --> 00:05:02.330
it just cut and paste it back in what really
irritates you about reading other screenplays

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:07.910
what irritates me is just when people haven't
done got it to where it should be at a level

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:11.199
of reading you know where they send you something
and they go oh don't read that wait I got

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:14.449
this one or I changed that or you or do you
read it you talk to them they're like oh I

00:05:14.449 --> 00:05:19.590
changed that it's like well that means you
know there was that famous Josh Olson article

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:24.659
[Deadline - 2009] which you can find online
about reading people's scripts which you know

00:05:24.659 --> 00:05:29.440
it's like if it's not ready to be read don't
send it to me because it's like like josh

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:33.599
had mentioned it's like asking somebody come
over to your house and paint for two hours

00:05:33.599 --> 00:05:37.960
you know you're taking up two hours of somebody's
time you know so don't give it to them to

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:43.509
read until it's like perfect and there you
know and if you want my notes and I give you

00:05:43.509 --> 00:05:47.630
some notes don't be defensive about it either
that's the most irritating thing it's like

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:51.340
don't say I want I want your opinion and then
you give somebody opinion or you say you need

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:56.729
to fix this okay great thanks and they're
they have an attitude about it it's like you

00:05:56.729 --> 00:06:03.449
know why'd you have me read it just tell you
it's great is it okay to question why you

00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:09.550
gave a certain note is that yeah oh yeah definitely
definitely yeah oh yeah of course and and

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:13.840
you know what do I know as much as I know
I don't know anything so it's like you know

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:19.319
sometimes my notes might not even be valid
it's just this is what I think you know but

00:06:19.319 --> 00:06:22.180
sometimes people don't even want to hear that
they just want they just want you to tell

00:06:22.180 --> 00:06:27.110
them it's great did you ever catch that in
yourself and really work on that it sounds

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:33.930
like you're okay with with negative feedback
it gets easier you know so I mean it all kind

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:38.930
of depends on who it comes from too it's like
you know I mean if if somebody reads a script

00:06:38.930 --> 00:06:44.039
of mine that's like never written a script
and they were like I don't like this and that

00:06:44.039 --> 00:06:49.009
I mean probably my knee jerk reaction like
what do you know but if it's a producer somebody

00:06:49.009 --> 00:06:54.720
who I respect and who's done a lot of work
then I listen to him but it does get easier

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:58.860
to take the negative feedback and like like
I said before it's about interpreting notes

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:04.150
too sometimes people can't explain why they
don't like something and you have to figure

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:13.550
that out would you say that 99 of what Hollywood
makes is garbage no there's some garbage but

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:20.360
no I think I think most films I mean most
films get started with very good intentions

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:24.139
and look I've made a few movies that I'm not
happy with either but the process is not a

00:07:24.139 --> 00:07:30.490
singular process you have to deal with producers
actors you know money guys distributors like

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:35.569
even the Nic Cage movie that I wrote it wasn't
called rage the distributor called it Rage

00:07:35.569 --> 00:07:40.279
you know it's like really Rage okay nobody's
gonna make fun of Rage in the Cage you know

00:07:40.279 --> 00:07:45.960
but I had no control over that so it you lose
so much control in the process that you know

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:52.400
it takes a very very strong director like
somebody like Quentin Tarantino who can say

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.619
no to everybody and filter out the bad that
comes along you know that's you know you're

00:07:57.619 --> 00:08:02.809
making you're doing art by committee so that's
why sometimes things they have great intentions

00:08:02.809 --> 00:08:06.619
maybe it was a great script maybe the actor
was amazing but things got messed up along

00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:11.809
the way because there's so many people's hands
on the editor you know the composer the director

00:08:11.809 --> 00:08:16.460
there's the screenwriter the producer who
shows up on set and wants to put a motorcycle

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:19.279
chase in the middle of the movie that makes
no sense and then you find out later it's

00:08:19.279 --> 00:08:24.309
because he can get a free BMW motorcycle if
we put him in the movie things like that happen

00:08:24.309 --> 00:08:30.279
so you know that's why sometimes movies don't
work out but I think most screenwriters and

00:08:30.279 --> 00:08:34.409
directors almost all of them have the intention
to make a great movies just things get in

00:08:34.409 --> 00:08:39.159
the way or lack of money or resources so if
someone's at a certain level then there's

00:08:39.159 --> 00:08:42.899
so much more creative control because they
can basically say no to people you need that

00:08:42.899 --> 00:08:47.259
like one per that's why score says you can
make but you need a strong director or writer

00:08:47.259 --> 00:08:52.839
director who can like no one's going to question
that because then it at least keeps everything

00:08:52.839 --> 00:08:56.740
focused there's very few of those people you
know but like I think Paul Thomas Anderson

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:59.990
I don't think any producers are going to show
up or money guys and tell them what to do

00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:04.750
you know so it's or Quentin Tarantino or Christopher
Nolan that's why they can make really great

00:09:04.750 --> 00:09:10.149
films because the white noise that's going
on around them they can just stop you know

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:14.639
or just ignore it or people just scared even
you know mess with them because people want

00:09:14.639 --> 00:09:18.529
to get their ideas into film too you'll have
you know money guys who you've never even

00:09:18.529 --> 00:09:22.870
met who gave some money on a film show up
and like give an idea and then other ones

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:27.259
are great and actually have good ideas so
you know there's just so many people that

00:09:27.259 --> 00:09:32.850
are involved in a film that it's hard sometimes
to keep it focused so I think that's the problem

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:37.020
with things that sometimes because there's
a lot of moves on Netflix you know look like

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:41.420
they had good intentions and like I said I've
i've worked on a few that like start off over

00:09:41.420 --> 00:09:43.060
here and ended up over there you know it happens.

00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:44.579
Question For The Viewers: What percentage
of the screenplays you read would you say

00:09:44.579 --> 00:09:44.720
